# Pocketwick Pin Map

This is the cheat sheet for what plugs into what on the Pocketwick main board (Rev E-M2). Keep it open while you're soldering or writing the software so you don't have to dig through the big manual.

Quick heads up: this is all based on the KiCad files. Nothing has been built yet, so if something on your real board doesn't match, trust a multimeter over this page.

---

## The Orange Pi's 40 pins

Pin 1 is the one with the square pad. Odd pins are on one row, even pins on the other, same as a Raspberry Pi.

The "Linux GPIO" number is what you use in software (like in the device tree or with `gpioget`). "Not used" means nothing on the board touches it, so it's free if you want to add stuff later.

| Pin | What it is on the Orange Pi | Linux GPIO | What Pocketwick uses it for |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 3.3V | | 3.3V power for the fuel gauge, clock and vibration modules |
| 2 | 5V | | 5V coming IN from the boost module (this powers the Orange Pi) |
| 3 | PB3 (I2C0 SDA) | 35 | I2C data line |
| 4 | 5V | | 5V from the boost module, same as pin 2 |
| 5 | PB2 (I2C0 SCL) | 34 | I2C clock line |
| 6 | GND | | Ground |
| 7 | PB4 (PWM) | 36 | Vibration driver trigger (DRV2605L IN pin) |
| 8 | PB9 (UART0 TX) | 41 | Debug serial TX, goes to J_DBG1 |
| 9 | GND | | Ground |
| 10 | PB10 (UART0 RX) | 42 | Debug serial RX, goes to J_DBG1 |
| 11 | PB0 | 32 | Not used |
| 12 | PB5 | 37 | **PWR_OFF**: Linux pulses this high right at the end of shutdown to cut the power |
| 13 | PB1 | 33 | Not used |
| 14 | GND | | Ground |
| 15 | PE11 | 139 | **PWR_SENSE**: goes HIGH while you're pressing the power button |
| 16 | PL2 | 354 | Not used |
| 17 | 3.3V | | 3.3V, same as pin 1 |
| 18 | PL3 | 355 | Not used |
| 19 | PE2 | 130 | Not used |
| 20 | GND | | Ground |
| 21 | PE3 | 131 | Not used |
| 22 | PD0 | 96 | Fuel gauge alert (low battery warning) |
| 23 | PE1 | 129 | Not used |
| 24 | PE0 | 128 | Not used |
| 25 | GND | | Ground |
| 26 | PE4 | 132 | Not used |
| 27 | PL5 | 357 | PG UP button (low when pressed) |
| 28 | PL4 | 356 | Clock module SQW output |
| 29 | PE12 | 140 | PG DOWN button (low when pressed) |
| 30 | GND | | Ground |
| 31 | PE13 | 141 | MENU button (low when pressed) |
| 32 | PD1 | 97 | Not used |
| 33 | PD3 | 99 | Not used |
| 34 | GND | | Ground |
| 35 | PB6 | 38 | Not used |
| 36 | PD2 | 98 | Not used |
| 37 | PD4 | 100 | Not used |
| 38 | PB8 | 40 | Not used |
| 39 | GND | | Ground |
| 40 | PB7 | 39 | Not used |

### Stuff to remember about the header

- **Pin 12 must stay low while the Orange Pi boots.** If it goes high during startup, the power switch thinks it's being told to turn off and kills everything. This hasn't been checked on a real board yet.
- **Pin 15 is the only button that's active HIGH.** The other three buttons (27, 29, 31) are active LOW, because they pull the pin to ground when you press them.
- Pins 2 and 4 are 5V going INTO the Orange Pi. Don't also plug a charger into the Orange Pi's own power port while it's docked.
- The I2C devices on pins 3 and 5 are at these addresses:
  - **0x36:** fuel gauge
  - **0x68:** clock
  - **0x5A:** vibration driver

